.\" UNIX V7 source code: see /COPYRIGHT or www.tuhs.org for details.
.TH ASSERT 3X
.SH NAME
assert, static_assert \-
program verification
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <assert.h>
.PP
.B void assert(scalar expression);
.PP
.B static_assert(constant-expression, string-literal);
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
.I Assert
is a macro that indicates
.I expression
is expected to be true at this point in the program.
It writes a diagnostic to the standard
.I error
file and calls
.IR abort (3)
when
.I expression
is false (0).
Compiling with the
.IR b6cc (1)
option
.SM
.B \-DNDEBUG
effectively deletes
.I assert
from the program.
.PP
.B Note:
v7's page said it "causes an exit(2) with a diagnostic comment on the standard
output".  Two corrections there:
.B the diagnostic goes to fd 2,
and the macro calls
.I abort
rather than
.BR exit(1) ,
which C11 sec 7.2.1.1 requires and which is worth having \- v7's
.B exit(1)
was indistinguishable from an ordinary failure.
.PP
.B Note:
.I static_assert
is C11 and had no v7 ancestor.  It is a macro spelling of the
.B _Static_assert
keyword, which the front end implements, so it costs nothing.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
`Assertion failed:
.I expression,
file
.I f,
line
.I n'
.PP
.B Note:
v7 printed the file and line only.
.I F
is the source file and
.I n
the source line number
of the
.I assert
statement.
.SH FILES
share/besm6/lib/libc.a
.br
lib/libc/gen/assert.c, include/assert.h
.SH SEE ALSO
abort(3)
.SH BESM-6 NOTES
.SS <assert.h> is deliberately unguarded, and it is the only such header here
C11 sec 7.2 says NDEBUG is re-examined at
.I every
inclusion, so a source may include the header, define NDEBUG, and include it
again to turn assertions off for the rest of the translation unit.  An include
guard would silently defeat that, so the file opens with
.B #undef assert
instead.
.SS assert is an expression
v7's macro expanded to a
.I brace block,
so
.B "if (p) assert(q); else ..."
was a syntax error and
.B "assert(q), f()"
would not parse at all.  C11 sec 7.2.1.1 requires a
.B void
expression, and this one is a conditional.
.PP
v7's macro also called
.I fprintf
and
.I exit
without declaring either, which no longer compiles: the front end has no implicit
declarations.
.SS The handler writes with write(), not through stdio
There is no stdio dependency here at all, and that is deliberate: an assertion
failure is exactly the moment not to depend on a buffered stream, which is the
thing most likely to be broken already.
.IR Perror (3)
writes the same way and for the same reason.
.PP
Nothing is #included by <assert.h> either \-
.B _Noreturn
is a keyword and the handler is declared in the header \- so a source that
asserts need not drag in stdio or stdlib to do it.
.SS _assert is kept
v7's alias, for the v7 sources that still spell it that way.
